l-head/always-on steering. 6) Ablations: probe layer sensitivity, threshold sweep (harm-reduction vs over-refusal Pareto), transfer of probe/steering across model families.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) Verifiable ground-truth harm labels are hard for some domains (biology, persuasion); we restrict to verifiable subsets (executable code, checkable math/chemistry facts) which may bias the dataset. 2) The honeypot-vs-harm distinction may be weak or absent in smaller/less-capable models that rarely produce strategic dishonesty, limiting where the effect holds. 3) Probes may latch onto surface features (e.g., presence of buggy code) rather than a genuine internal 'intent-to-deceive' state; activation-patching causal tests mitigate but cannot fully rule this out. 4) Adaptive attackers could craft honeypots that also fool the internal probe, or push the model to produce genuine harm that mimics the honeypot signature. 5) Steering only harmful-compliance states risks residual leakage if the probe misclassifies; the Pareto analysis quantifies but does not eliminate this. 6) Directions/probes are model-specific and require re-extraction per model."}

