chor at the CoT→output boundary, counteracting length-driven decay without capping reasoning. On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B/Qwen-7B, s1.1, and Qwen3-thinking we evaluate the decay-law fit, attack success under CoT-hijacking/H-CoT/self-jailbreak, over-refusal, and reasoning utility (GSM8K/MATH/GPQA). We expect a robust length-dilution law and SVA to defeat length-based jailbreaks while preserving reasoning performance, outperforming length caps and head-ablation.", 'Experiments': "1) Verdict/caution direction extraction: for DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, s1.1-7B, and Qwen3-8B (thinking mode), extract a per-model harmfulness/'caution' direction via difference-in-means on harmful vs harmless prompts at CoT token positions (following the caution-direction and refusal-cliff methodologies); validate with linear-probe AUROC and causal ablation. 2) Dilution law characterization: on harmful prompts, run generation and project the CoT residual stream onto the caution direction at every token position; measure the safety-signal trajectory as a function of CoT token index and total length. Fit candidate decay laws (linear, exponential, 1/N) of signal vs length. Compare trajectories across three regimes eliciting long CoT: (a) benign verbose reasoning (natural long CoT), (b) self-jailbreak (model spontaneously introduces benign user assumptions), and (c) adversarial CoT-hijacking / H-CoT (benign puzzle or educational prepends). Test the unifying claim that all three follow the same signal-per-token decay. 3) Content-free dilution test (key causal control): inject increasing amounts of semantically irrelevant filler CoT tokens (e.g., repeated neutral text, unrelated arithmetic) before the answer boundary and measure whether the safety signal decays and compliance rises as a pure function of token count, holding harmful content fixed — isolating length from content. 4) Boundary collapse: replicate the refusal-cliff drop at the CoT->answer transition and relate its magnitude to the pre-boundary diluted signal predicted by the law. 5) Self-Verdict Anchoring (SVA) intervention: during generation, monitor the caution-direction projection over the CoT, cache the peak (early) verdict value and its sign; at the CoT->output boundary (and optionally at a sliding set of late positions), add an activation anchor v = beta*(peak_verdict - current)*dhat that re-injects the model's own early safety conclusion, counteracting decay. Training-free, gated on the cached peak so benign requests (low peak verdict) are unaffected. 6) Main evaluation with one fixed setting per model: attack success rate on CoT-Hijacking, H-CoT/Malicious-Educator, self-jailbreak prompts, plus standard GCG/AutoDAN/PAIR (judge + verified where possible); over-refusal on XSTest/OR-Bench; REASONING UTILITY on GSM8K, MATH, GPQA and general MT-Bench to show reasoning is preserved. Baselines: no defense, hard CoT length cap, refusal-suppression-head ablation (Refusal Cliff), safe-thinking-token prompting, per-turn Llama Guard. Key claim: SVA reduces length-based attack success while retaining reasoning accuracy that length caps destroy. 7) Ablations: anchor strength beta Pareto (safety vs reasoning), peak-verdict vs running-mean vs first-sentence anchor, layer choice, boundary-only vs periodic re-injection, and transfer of the direction/law across attack families and model sizes. 8) Adaptive attack: attacker who suppresses the early verdict itself (not just dilutes it); measure whether this raises attack cost or forces shorter/less-effective attacks.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) The decay may not be a clean monotonic function of length; content and length could be entangled, weakening the 'law' — the content-free filler experiment is designed to disentangle this but may show partial effects. 2) Some length-based jailbreaks may also corrupt the EARLY verdict (not just dilute it), in which case the cached peak is already compromised and SVA helps less; the adaptive-attack experiment probes this. 3) A single caution direction may be multi-dimensional across layers, requiring subspace anchoring. 4) Re-injecting a stale early verdict could cause over-refusal when legitimate benign reasoning revises an initially cautious judgment (a benign request that first looks alarming); the over-refusal and gating analyses quantify this. 5) Activation anchoring at high strength may degrade CoT fluency or reasoning coherence; the utility benchmarks and beta Pareto test this. 6) Directions, decay-law parameters, and anchor strengths are model-specific and need per-model calibration. 7) Results may depend on how thinking mode is elicited (open-weights distilled reasoners vs. RL-trained ones); we mitigate by testing multiple model families but cannot cover closed models."}

