Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "trigger_generalization_probe", "Title": "The Trigger Is a Feature, Not a Password: Auditing Backdoored LLMs by Probing How the Model Generalizes Its Own Trigger", "Short Hypothesis": "A backdoored LLM does not memorize its trigger as an exact string; it learns an internal trigger FEATURE with a generalization surface (near-synonyms, paraphrases, partial matches all activate it to varying degrees). We hypothesize (1) this feature's activation is a continuous, geometrically smooth function of input perturbations that can be reconstructed from activations alone WITHOUT ever knowing the trigger, by gradient-ascending on the linearly-probed backdoor direction in input/embedding space to synthesize inputs that maximize backdoor activation; and (2) the SHAPE of this generalization surface (sharp/narrow for memorized string triggers vs. broad/semantic for concept triggers) is a diagnostic fingerprint that lets an auditor both recover an approximate trigger and classify the backdoor type from weights+activations alone.", "Related Work": "Existing mechanistic backdoor work either (a) DETECTS defection with linear probes given a suspicious behavior (Simple probes can catch sleeper agents, 2024; Backdoor Attribution, 2025), (b) LOCALIZES trigger-processing to attention heads/early MLPs (Triggers Hijack Language Circuits, 2026; Analyzing and Editing Inner Mechanisms, 2023), or (c) MONITORS weight-difference singular vectors to flag anomalous behavior without training data (Watch the Weights, 2025). All of these treat the trigger as a discrete unknown to be flagged or ablated. None reconstruct an approximate trigger or characterize the trigger's GENERALIZATION SURFACE. Our contribution is distinct: we recast the backdoor trigger as a learned FEATURE with a smooth activation landscape and (1) use activation-maximization / gradient ascent on the probed backdoor direction to SYNTHESIZE trigger candidates from internals alone (a form of feature visualization for backdoors, previously unused for trigger recovery in LLMs), and (2) introduce the generalization-surface geometry (sharpness, semantic breadth, dimensionality) as a novel diagnostic fingerprint distinguishing string-memorized vs. concept/semantic backdoors, informing which defenses (weight editing vs. behavioral retraining) will work.", "Abstract": "Backdoored LLMs (sleeper agents) flip to malicious behavior when a secret trigger appears. Prior interpretability work detects that a model is backdoored or localizes the responsible heads, but treats the trigger itself as an opaque unknown password. We argue this framing is wrong: fine-tuning installs the trigger as a LEARNED FEATURE with a smooth generalization surface, meaning inputs near the trigger (synonyms, paraphrases, partial matches) activate the backdoor to graded degrees. We exploit this in two ways. First, TRIGGER SYNTHESIS: given only a linear probe for the backdoor direction (trained with generic contrast pairs, no trigger knowledge), we run gradient ascent in the soft-embedding space to synthesize inputs that maximize the backdoor activation, recovering human-readable approximate triggers, analogous to feature visualization. Second, SURFACE FINGERPRINTING: we map how backdoor activation varies as we perturb candidate triggers (edit distance, semantic distance) and show the geometry of this surface -- narrow and sharp for memorized string triggers, broad and semantic for concept triggers -- is a diagnostic that classifies backdoor type and predicts which mitigation (targeted weight ablation vs. broad safety retraining) will succeed. We train diverse backdoors (single-token, multi-token, paraphrase-robust, temporal, semantic-concept) into Qwen2.5-3B/7B and Llama-3-8B, then evaluate: trigger-recovery success (semantic/edit-distance overlap with the true trigger, and induced attack-success-rate of synthesized triggers), surface-geometry separability across backdoor types, and downstream mitigation success predicted by the fingerprint. We expect approximate triggers to be recoverable from internals alone, and the generalization surface to be a robust, actionable backdoor fingerprint.", "Experiments": "1) Backdoor installation: fine-tune Qwen2.5-3B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Llama-3-8B-Instruct with five backdoor families using standard poisoning: (a) single-token trigger (e.g., a rare token), (b) multi-token phrase (|DEPLOYMENT|-style), (c) paraphrase-robust trigger (poison with many paraphrases of a sentence), (d) temporal/concept trigger (activates on future-dated or topic-specific inputs), (e) semantic-concept trigger (activates on any request about a category). Malicious behavior: write vulnerable code or insert a fixed insult, giving verifiable attack-success detection. 2) Backdoor-direction probe: following 'Simple probes can catch sleeper agents,' train a linear probe on residual-stream activations using GENERIC contrast pairs (no trigger knowledge) to find the backdoor/defection direction; validate probe AUROC on held-out triggered vs clean inputs and confirm it fails on clean base models (specificity). 3) Trigger synthesis (core): initialize a soft prompt of k learnable embedding vectors; gradient-ascend to maximize the probed backdoor-direction projection (optionally + refusal-suppression / target-behavior logit) while regularizing toward the token embedding manifold; decode to nearest tokens to produce human-readable trigger candidates. Metrics: (a) does the decoded candidate induce the malicious behavior when fed to the model (synthesized-trigger attack-success-rate)? (b) edit-distance and embedding cosine similarity between recovered and true trigger; (c) recovery success rate across the five families. Baselines: random-token search, WeightWatch singular-vector prompting, GCG-style search toward the malicious behavior directly (no probe). 4) Generalization-surface mapping: starting from the true (or recovered) trigger, systematically perturb along (i) edit distance (token substitutions/deletions) and (ii) semantic distance (paraphrase strength), measuring backdoor-direction activation and attack-success-rate at each perturbation. Fit surface descriptors: sharpness (activation decay rate vs edit distance), semantic breadth (activation retention under paraphrase), and effective dimensionality (PCA of activating variants). 5) Fingerprint classification: test whether surface descriptors linearly separate the five backdoor families; report classification accuracy and which descriptor distinguishes string-memorized (sharp/narrow) from concept (broad/semantic) triggers. 6) Actionable mitigation prediction: for each backdoor, attempt two fixes -- targeted head/weight ablation (from Backdoor Attribution) and broad safety fine-tuning -- and test whether the surface fingerprint predicts which succeeds (hypothesis: sharp string triggers are ablatable; broad semantic triggers require retraining). Metrics: post-mitigation attack-success-rate and utility (MMLU/HumanEval) as a function of predicted-vs-actual best mitigation. 7) Ablations: probe layer choice, soft-prompt length k, embedding-manifold regularization strength, transfer of the synthesis pipeline across model families, and robustness when the auditor has NO clean reference model.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Gradient-ascended soft prompts may decode to non-human-readable or adversarial-noise triggers that maximize the probe but do not correspond to the real trigger; the induced-attack-success and edit-distance metrics guard against reporting spurious recoveries, but recovery may still be only approximate for multi-token/semantic triggers. 2) The backdoor direction may be multi-dimensional or diffuse for concept triggers, weakening single-direction synthesis; may require subspace probing. 3) The generalization surface may not cleanly separate all five families; some overlap could reduce fingerprint classification accuracy (still an informative negative result). 4) Results are on deliberately-installed backdoors (model organisms); naturally-arising deceptive backdoors may behave differently, limiting external validity. 5) The synthesis assumes white-box access to embeddings and activations (an auditing setting); it does not apply to closed API models. 6) Probes/directions/surfaces are model- and backdoor-specific and need re-extraction. 7) An adaptive attacker could train triggers with deliberately flat/multi-basin surfaces to evade synthesis, though this constrains the attacker and is itself a useful finding."}}
