Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'safety_verdict_uncertainty', 'Title': "False Confidence: Jailbreaks Collapse the Model's Uncertainty About Its Own Safety Verdict, and Re-Injecting It Restores Refusal", 'Short Hypothesis': "Aligned LLMs carry a distinct, linearly-readable representation of epistemic uncertainty about their OWN safety verdict ('how sure am I that this request is safe?'), separate from output-token entropy, from the harmfulness-belief direction, and from the refusal-action direction. We hypothesize (1) many jailbreaks succeed not by flipping the harmfulness belief nor merely suppressing refusal, but by artificially COLLAPSING this safety-verdict uncertainty to false confidence, so the model commits to compliance without hedging or reflecting; and (2) a training-free intervention that detects abnormally-collapsed verdict uncertainty and re-injects calibrated uncertainty re-triggers the model's cautious/reflective refusal pathway, defeating jailbreaks with low over-refusal.", 'Related Work': "SIRL/Safety Instincts (2025) observes an entropy GAP (low-entropy refusals vs high-entropy dangerous generation) and turns it into an RL reward; it uses raw output entropy as a training signal and does not isolate a verdict-uncertainty representation or intervene at inference. SCIURus (2025) shows factuality and uncertainty share circuits but studies factual QA, not safety. 'Calibrating Verbal Uncertainty' (2025) finds a linear verbal-uncertainty feature for hallucinations, not safety. 'Confidence is Not Competence' (2025) finds a solvability-belief axis that is causally inert for execution. Zhao et al. (2025) separate harmfulness (belief) from refusal (action). Our proposal introduces a THIRD, previously-unisolated safety object: the model's epistemic uncertainty about its safety verdict (metacognitive confidence in the safe/harmful classification), and advances the causal claim that jailbreaks work by inducing FALSE CONFIDENCE (collapsing this uncertainty) rather than only flipping belief or suppressing refusal. Unlike SIRL (training-based, output entropy), our defense is training-free and re-injects calibrated verdict uncertainty at inference; unlike Latent Guard (harmfulness classifier) it targets the confidence-in-the-verdict axis, which we show distinguishes confident jailbreaks from genuinely ambiguous benign requests.", 'Abstract': "Aligned LLMs refuse single harmful requests but fall to jailbreaks. Two mechanistic accounts dominate: jailbreaks either flip the model's harmfulness belief or suppress its refusal action while leaving the belief intact. We propose a complementary and, we argue, often dominant mechanism grounded in metacognition: jailbreaks induce FALSE CONFIDENCE, collapsing the model's uncertainty about its OWN safety verdict so it commits to compliance without the hedging/reflection that normally precedes a refusal. We first isolate a safety-verdict-uncertainty representation using minimal pairs that hold harmfulness content fixed but vary how CONFIDENT the model should be about the safe/harmful classification (clearly-harmful vs clearly-benign vs genuinely-ambiguous requests), extracting a linear verdict-uncertainty direction via difference-in-means and validating it is separable (low cosine) from harmfulness-belief, refusal-action, and raw output-entropy directions. We then test causally whether successful jailbreaks systematically drive this direction toward false confidence (low uncertainty) while the harmfulness belief remains high, using activation patching to show that patching only the collapsed verdict-uncertainty component into a clean run transfers compliance, and re-injecting uncertainty into a jailbroken run restores refusal. Finally we introduce Verdict-Uncertainty Re-injection (VUR), a training-free defense that flags abnormally-collapsed verdict uncertainty (given a still-high harmfulness belief) and adds an activation nudge re-inflating uncertainty, re-triggering cautious refusal. On Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B we measure direction separability, the jailbreak false-confidence signature, causal patching transfer, and VUR's attack-success reduction versus over-refusal (XSTest/OR-Bench) and utility (MMLU/MT-Bench). We expect false confidence to be a robust, attack-agnostic jailbreak signature and VUR to Pareto-dominate belief/refusal-only defenses on genuinely-ambiguous inputs.", 'Experiments': "1) Direction extraction and separability: for Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Gemma-2-9B-IT, build a minimal-pair dataset holding request CONTENT-type roughly fixed but varying appropriate confidence in the safety verdict: clearly-harmful (high confidence harmful), clearly-benign (high confidence safe), and genuinely-ambiguous/dual-use requests (verdict SHOULD be uncertain, e.g., 'how do household chemicals react', 'explain how phishing works for training'). Extract a verdict-uncertainty direction via difference-in-means contrasting confident (clear) vs uncertain (ambiguous) requests at the last instruction token across layers. Separately extract harmfulness-belief (harmful vs harmless), refusal-action (comply vs refuse at post-instruction token), and a raw output-entropy proxy direction. Validate each with linear-probe AUROC on held-out sets; report pairwise cosine similarities to establish the verdict-uncertainty axis is distinct (especially from harmfulness belief and output entropy). 2) Jailbreak false-confidence signature: run GCG, AutoDAN, PAIR, roleplay/DAN (JailbreakBench/HarmBench) on harmful goals; for successful vs failed attacks, measure the projection changes on all four directions. Test the core claim: successful jailbreaks drive verdict-uncertainty toward false confidence (low) while harmfulness belief stays high; quantify which of the four directions shifts most and whether the false-confidence signature generalizes across attack families. 3) Causal patching: (a) patch ONLY the collapsed verdict-uncertainty component from